name: Telecom bypass (SIM-box)
tagline: "SIM-box detection from voice CDRs - twelve daily per-SIM signals, an explainable Risk Score, an allow list with expiry, persistence and shadow mode, and one Incident and Case per suspected box, over a synthetic corpus"
description: "A bypass / SIM-box detection content pack (slice B3 of the plan: signals, score, lists, the SIM Alert and the box Incident and Case). One voice CDR feed with optional device and call-outcome columns, three small Reference feeds (cell sites, own number ranges, the subscriber profile) and six nightly Jobs. tb_features writes one row per SIM per day with twelve CDR-derivable signals (B-number diversity, call shape, answer rate, inbound share, service mix, cells and implied speed, sequential dialling, hour profile, burst cadence, overlapping calls, own-network targets, tenure and activation batch) plus a box_like flag from five configurable clauses; a signal whose optional column or Reference is absent is omitted and named in the missing column, never a failure. tb_factors turns those signals into the factor columns of the tb_sim Risk Score (eight weighted signals, three protective factors that subtract, 0 to 100, every score recomputable from its stored factors); the risk.score Job tb_risk_score scores each SIM; tb_findings applies agreement (enough primary signals), persistence (high on enough of the last nights), the tb_allow Entity List (verified lines, with an expiry), the tb_watch Entity List (a watched SIM needs one night fewer) and a shadow switch, and writes the Dataset the Alert Rule tb_sim_high reads. A second Alert Rule, tb_allowed_line_changed, flags an allowed line whose shape has changed. Two more Jobs find the box: tb_cluster_members keeps the candidate SIMs (the same five clauses at a lower volume floor, so a slow-roll box whose SIMs are each under the SIM-day floor is still found, minus allowed lines) that share a device (IMEI), a cell or an activation batch with enough other candidates, and tb_clusters rolls them up to one row per suspected box; the Alert Rule tb_box_suspected opens one Incident per box, the Case Rule tb_box_case groups it into a Case, and the Link Analysis view tb_box_members shows its SIMs as one graph. The Entity Lists themselves cannot ship in a template: create tb_allow and tb_watch (the admin guide gives the calls); until then the Jobs read empty lists. The Anomaly Model factor and the 7-day device signals arrive in later slices. A CDR pattern is evidence of consistency with a box, never proof. Every threshold and weight is configuration and illustrative until calibrated on the operator's own traffic. Needs the Standard, Advanced or Preview edition."
minEdition: standard
icon: heroicons_outline:signal-slash
contents[10]: "4 Pipelines + schemas (voice CDR, cell sites, number plan, subscriber profile)", "5 nightly sql.template Jobs (tb_features, tb_factors, tb_findings, tb_cluster_members, tb_clusters) and 1 risk.score Job (tb_risk_score)", "tb_sim Risk Score (11 factors, 3 protective)", "3 Alert Rules (tb_box_suspected, tb_sim_high, tb_allowed_line_changed), a Case Rule, a Link Analysis view and a runbook per box", "9 Datasets (4 feeds, 5 Job-written)", "Zero-row schema seeds and Entity List sidecar seeds (tb_allow, tb_watch)", "Allow list with expiry, watch list, persistence, agreement and shadow mode", "Synthetic corpus with planted boxes and look-alikes", "Runbook skeleton", "Example SLA policies and Escalation Rules (inert)"
